  <data key="d3">chunk-35a8aa10543865bc8c411749cbcf05e4</data>
  <data key="d4">attention.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1763834865</data>
</node>
<node id="Figure_2_Scaled_DotProduct_and_MultiHead_Attention_diagram_from_Transformer_paper (image)">
  <data key="d0">Figure_2_Scaled_DotProduct_and_MultiHead_Attention_diagram_from_Transformer_paper (image)</data>
  <data key="d1">image</data>
  <data key="d2">Technical two-panel schematic: left panel shows Scaled Dot-Product Attention (Q, K, V → QK^T / sqrt(d_k) → softmax → weighted V, with optional masking); right panel shows Multi-Head Attention (learned linear projections W_i^Q/K/V → parallel attention heads → concatenation → output projection W^O). The figure visually maps to Sections 3.2 and 3.2.2, illustrating scaling, masking, and parallel attention heads.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-1b9aaf36d59f2f0347d9d1ffa8df6d4b</data>
  <data key="d4">attention.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1763834865</data>
</node>
<node id="Scaled dot-product attention (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Scaled dot-product attention (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Defines the Transformer attention operator: compute dot-product scores QK^T, scale by 1/√d_k, apply row-wise softmax to obtain attention weights, and form weighted sums of V. It produces differentiable, normalized context vectors used throughout encoder/decoder attention, stabilizes gradients for large dimensions, and enables parallelizable modeling of pairwise interactions in sequence data.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-07ebbd960aadcc1611f6b1a2d7759f19</data>
  <data key="d4">attention.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1763834865</data>
</node>
<node id="Statistical justification for scaling in Scaled Dot-Product Attention (discarded fragment) (discarded)">
  <data key="d0">Statistical justification for scaling in Scaled Dot-Product Attention (discarded fragment) (discarded)</data>
  <data key="d1">discarded</data>
  <data key="d2">A short probabilistic derivation assuming i.i.d. components (mean 0, variance 1) of q and k showing q·k = sum_i q_i k_i has mean 0 and variance d_k; supports the paper's motivation to scale dot products by 1/sqrt(d_k) to avoid large magnitudes and softmax saturation.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ebd33f4b91ec138e76e84ffd2c9ec597</data>
  <data key="d4">attention.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1763834865</data>
</node>
<node id="page-number_footer_digit_page4 (discarded)">
  <data key="d0">page-number_footer_digit_page4 (discarded)</data>
  <data key="d1">discarded</data>
  <data key="d2">A single-digit discarded token "4" with bbox [493,935,504,946] on page_idx 3, most likely a pagination/footer mark (printed page 4). It is a layout artifact with no semantic relevance to the Transformer content and should be preserved only as layout metadata for document reconstruction.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-ba07431d87fe04e97deb0705c166f15b</data>
  <data key="d4">attention.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1763834865</data>
</node>
<node id="Multi-Head Attention (Transformer) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Multi-Head Attention (Transformer) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">Defines the Transformer multi-head attention: project queries, keys, and values into h subspaces, apply scaled dot-product attention in each (head_i = Attention(QW_i^Q, KW_i^K, VW_i^V)), concatenate the h head outputs, and linearly project with W^O to produce the final d_model-dimensional output. This enables parallel, multi-subspace attention and stabilizes softmax gradients via 1/√d_k scaling.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-71b0550271fdf0a898cd35e5d8b2fd23</data>
  <data key="d4">attention.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1763834865</data>
</node>
<node id="Position-wise feed-forward network (FFN) in the Transformer (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Position-wise feed-forward network (FFN) in the Transformer (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">A two-layer position-wise MLP: project input x from d_model to a higher-dimensional hidden space d_ff via an affine map, apply ReLU elementwise, then project back to d_model. It provides per-position nonlinearity and increased capacity in Transformer layers, is implemented as two 1×1 convolutions, and is applied identically and independently at each sequence position.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-f3a8f45137f2da168a9dc7fe376fa093</data>
  <data key="d4">attention.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1763834865</data>
</node>
<node id="page_footer_pagination_token_5 (discarded)">
  <data key="d0">page_footer_pagination_token_5 (discarded)</data>
  <data key="d1">discarded</data>
  <data key="d2">A single-character pagination/OCR artifact ('5') located in the page footer (bbox [493,935,504,946] on page_idx 4). Useful for pagination/layout metadata; not semantically relevant to the Transformer content and should be excluded from semantic indexing.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-3468be2d64ebbef98ab8be50f6aae008</data>
  <data key="d4">attention.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1763834865</data>
</node>
<node id="Table 1: Complexity, Parallelism and Maximum Path Length Comparison of Layer Types (table)">
  <data key="d0">Table 1: Complexity, Parallelism and Maximum Path Length Comparison of Layer Types (table)</data>
  <data key="d1">table</data>
  <data key="d2">Compares four layer types (Self-Attention, Recurrent, Convolutional, Self-Attention restricted) by per-layer computational complexity, minimum sequential operations, and maximum path length. Shows self-attention yields O(1) sequential operations and O(1) path length but O(n^2·d) compute, while recurrent layers are O(n·d^2) with O(n) serial steps and O(n) path length. The table supports the paper's argument favoring self-attention for parallelism and short dependency paths (max 100 words).</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-701d1b288248a1b622507aeb1a436649</data>
  <data key="d4">attention.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1763834865</data>
</node>
<node id="Sinusoidal positional encoding (Transformer) (equation)">
  <data key="d0">Sinusoidal positional encoding (Transformer) (equation)</data>
  <data key="d1">equation</data>
  <data key="d2">A deterministic mapping that encodes token positions into d_model-dimensional vectors using paired sine and cosine functions at geometrically spaced frequencies. These encodings are summed with token embeddings to provide absolute and linearizable relative positional information for the Transformer's attention-based architecture, enabling order-awareness without recurrence.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-53601a6e0197c4b0bdb59fd1b8fbbc03</data>
  <data key="d4">attention.pdf</data>
  <data key="d5">1763834865</data>
</node>
